Declarative vs. Imperative Knowledge
​Concept Comparison
​DECLARATIVE · "THE DRAWING"  
​Definition: A statement of fact — what is true. It describes the finished state but tells you nothing about how to reach it.  
​Example: "The shaft diameter is 25.00 \pm 0.05\text{ mm}." True, but useless for machining it.  
​IMPERATIVE · "THE ROUTE SHEET"  
​Definition: A recipe — how to do it. A sequence of steps that, followed exactly, produces the fact.  
​Example: "Face the end \rightarrow rough turn to 25.4 \rightarrow finish turn to 25.05 \rightarrow grind to size." Follow it, get the shaft.  
​The Punchline: A computer program is imperative knowledge written so precisely that a machine with no common sense can follow it. Every drawing needs a route sheet; every "what" needs a "how". Programming is route-sheet writing for facts.
  
